"# Decomposing a Research Question on a Major Event: A Structured Set of Focused Sub-Questions\n\n**Scope note:** Because the parent research question concerns an unspecified major event (disaster, battle, attack, or catastrophe), the decomposition below is presented as a general-purpose, MECE-compliant template organized around the four requested dimensions — **causes, key events, casualties, and aftermath**. It can be instantiated for any specific event by substituting the event's name, date range, and location into each sub-question.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Methodological Basis for the Decomposition\n\nThe structure follows established guidance on research-question decomposition:\n\n- **MECE logic** — sub-questions should be *Mutually Exclusive* (no overlapping facets) and *Collectively Exhaustive* (answering all of them resolves the parent question), with each branch justified by an explicit logic link back to the parent question [8][9].\n- **The funnel refinement process** — broad topic → literature scan → parent question → analytical framework → sub-questions → feasibility check, with the logic chain documented so it remains auditable [1][12][13].\n- **Historical-inquiry scaffolding** — the conventional layered structure for major events: *underlying causes → immediate triggers → course of events → consequences*, formalized since Fay's WWI study and traceable to Thucydides' separation of stated grievances from the \"truest cause\" [1][2].\n- **Temporal layering** — Braudel's three durations (*longue durée*, conjonctures, événements) justify separating structural, intermediate, and trigger-level causes [3].\n- **Ordering convention** — sub-questions should progress from descriptive to relational to causal [10].\n\nThe resulting set contains **16 focused sub-questions in four clusters**, within the recommended cap of 3–7 per dimension [10][12].\n\n---\n\n## 2. Cluster A — Causes (4 sub-questions)\n\nThe causes cluster applies the standard historical distinction between long-term/structural causes, medium-term/intermediate causes, and short-term triggers [1][3], plus hierarchy-of-causation logic (necessary vs. sufficient, proximate vs. distal, structural vs. intentional) [4][5][6].\n\n> **A1. (Structural)** What long-term political, economic, social, environmental, or technological conditions created the vulnerability or tension that made the event possible?\n> *Rationale:* Braudel's *longue durée* — deep structures that predate and outlast the event [3]; Wisner et al.'s Pressure-and-Release model locates \"root causes\" upstream of any trigger [22].\n\n> **A2. (Intermediate)** What medium-term developments (policy decisions, institutional failures, escalatory dynamics, resource pressures) in the months or years before the event intensified those conditions?\n> *Rationale:* The *conjonctures* layer of Braudel's model; Carr's insistence that the historian *ranks* causes rather than merely listing them [3][4].\n\n> **A3. (Trigger)** What specific proximate event or decision served as the immediate trigger, and why did *this* trigger prove potent when earlier similar incidents did not?\n> *Rationale:* The trigger converts latent tension into open rupture (e.g., Sarajevo and the July Crisis) [1][7]; potency is explained by the structures that made it so.\n\n> **A4. (Agency and contingency)** Which actors' decisions, organizational processes, or systemic failures were decisive, and how much of the outcome was contingent versus structurally determined?\n> *Rationale:* Allison's three lenses (rational actor, organizational process, bureaucratic politics) for dissecting decision windows [8]; Clark's contingency framing [7]; counterfactual reasoning to test whether identified causes were truly necessary [16].\n\n---\n\n## 3. Cluster B — Key Events (4 sub-questions)\n\nThis cluster reconstructs the course of events using chronological reconstruction, phase analysis, and turning-point/path-dependence concepts [9][10][11][12].\n\n> **B1. (Chronology)** What is the verified timeline of the event, from first precursor signals through termination — and where do sources disagree on timing or sequence?\n> *Rationale:* Source criticism (sourcing, contextualization, corroboration) is the operational core of event reconstruction [9][10].\n\n> **B2. (Phases)** What distinct phases can the event be divided into (e.g., warning, onset, escalation, climax, de-escalation), and what marked each transition?\n> *Rationale:* Brinton's stage analysis as a template for phase division [11]; Collingwood's \"inside and outside of the event\" requires reconstructing both the physical occurrence and the reasoning behind it [13].\n\n> **B3. (Turning points)** Which moments were genuine turning points in the path-dependent sense — decisions or occurrences that foreclosed some options and enabled others?\n> *Rationale:* Critical junctures and path dependence (Pierson) explain how early events constrain later ones [12].\n\n> **B4. (Response during the event)** How did authorities, organizations, and affected populations act *during* the event, and what proportion of the outcome is attributable to agent-generated versus response-generated problems?\n> *Rationale:* Quarantelli's distinction shows most response dysfunction is organizational (coordination, communication), not physical [5]; Comfort's self-organization lens covers emergent multi-actor behavior [6].\n\n---\n\n## 4. Cluster C — Casualties (4 sub-questions)\n\nCasualty sub-questions are the most methodologically sensitive: research shows order-of-magnitude divergence between methods (e.g., Iraq: ~60k–87k documented minimum vs. ~151k survey estimate vs. ~655k excess-death estimate [2][3][4]) and recurring over/undercount driven by definitions, access, and incentives. Each sub-question below embeds the triangulation standard: **lists/counts → verified minimum; surveys/excess models → plausible total with interval; forensics/OSINT → spot verification** [1][2][5][13].\n\n> **C1. (Definitions)** How are casualties defined for this event — direct vs. indirect deaths, civilian vs. combatant, injured vs. killed, and what inclusion thresholds apply?\n> *Rationale:* Definitional divergence is a dominant driver of discrepancy (e.g., DRC's ~5.4M figure was mostly indirect deaths and heavily contested; EM-DAT requires ≥10 deaths, UCDP ≥25 battle deaths) [1][9].\n\n> **C2. (Direct toll and sources)** What is the best-supported count of direct deaths and injuries, and what is the evidentiary basis (body counts, hospital records, named-victim lists, civil registration)?\n> *Rationale:* Each source type has known systematic weaknesses — morgue counts miss unrecovered bodies; hospital records miss pre-hospital deaths; media counts are floors, not totals [5][2][13].\n\n> **C3. (Indirect and total burden)** What do excess-mortality, survey, or demographic-inference methods suggest about the *total* death toll including indirect deaths (disease, displacement, collapsed care), and how wide is the uncertainty interval?\n> *Rationale:* Excess-mortality and cluster-survey methods capture unreported deaths but are baseline-sensitive (e.g., WHO's 14.9M COVID excess deaths vs. 5.4M reported; Lancet Iraq CI 426k–794k) [3][6]; multiple-systems estimation (e.g., Peru TRC's ~69,280) models the unseen from list overlap [14].\n\n> **C4. (Discrepancies and revision trajectory)** How have casualty figures changed over time, what explains initial over- or undercounts (duplicate reports, missing-persons misclassification, political incentives, infrastructure collapse), and what is the current consensus range?\n> *Rationale:* Tolls typically drift upward for weeks (2004 tsunami: ~228k final) but can fall after deduplication (9/11: initial ~6,000+ reports → final 2,977); Hurricane Maria went from 64 official to 2,975 excess [7][8]; best practice is to report point estimate *with* range, methodology, definition, and as-of date, avoiding false precision [1][2][5].\n\n---\n\n## 5. Cluster D — Aftermath (4 sub-questions)\n\nThe aftermath cluster applies the Haas–Kates–Aydelotte phase model (emergency → restoration → replacement → commemorative reconstruction) as its backbone [1], sorted across short-term response and long-term political, economic, social, and memorialization effects.\n\n> **D1. (Short-term response)** How effective was the emergency response — mobilization speed, casualty management, shelter/lifeline restoration, inter-agency coordination — and how did leadership perform on sense-making, decision-making, meaning-making, terminating, and learning?\n> *Rationale:* Boin et al.'s five crisis-leadership tasks; Tierney/Lindell/Perry's organizational performance criteria [4][7]; the commemorative phase of the reconstruction model is where memorialization formally enters the sequence [1].\n\n> **D2. (Political effects)** Did the event function as a \"focusing event\" that opened policy windows, reshaped agendas, and drove blame attribution — and did it accelerate political opening or authoritarian closure?\n> *Rationale:* Birkland's focusing-events theory [8]; Pelling & Dill's tipping-points framework (Nicaragua 1972, Mexico City 1985 as opening cases) [10]; Olson & Drury on disasters redistributing power along pre-existing fault lines [9]; Klein's disaster-capitalism critique as the counterpoint lens [11].\n\n> **D3. (Economic and social effects)** What were the direct (stock) and indirect (output) economic losses, how were they distributed across social groups, and what do mental-health, social-capital, and displacement data show about long-term social recovery?\n> *Rationale:* Cavallo & Noy's direct/indirect loss distinction [14]; Hallegatte et al.'s socioeconomic-resilience measure (welfare losses to the poor, not asset losses) [16]; Aldrich on social capital predicting recovery speed better than aid volume [23]; Norris et al. and Kaniasty & Norris on psychopathology and social-support deterioration [18][19]; Erikson's \"collective trauma\" as damage to the social fabric itself [20].\n\n> **D4. (Memory and reconstruction)** How has the event been memorialized (or obliterated), what narratives won the meaning contest, and did reconstruction follow \"build back better\" principles or reproduce pre-event vulnerabilities?\n> *Rationale:* Foote's four site-processes (sanctification, designation, rectification, obliteration) [31]; Alexander et al.'s cultural-trauma model of carrier groups competing over meaning [29]; Vale & Campanella on reconstruction as political narrative [32]; Sendai/PDNA \"Build Back Better\" standards [38][39]; Alesch et al.'s caution that the \"recovery-as-opportunity\" narrative is empirically weak — recovery is long, uneven, and household-level [43].\n\n---\n\n## 6. Quality Checks Applied to the Set\n\n| Criterion | How the set satisfies it | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Mutually exclusive** | Each cluster addresses one dimension; within clusters, sub-questions are separated by temporal layer (A1–A3), phase (B2–B3), method (C1–C4), or domain (D2–D4) | [8][9] |\n| **Collectively exhaustive** | Answering A1–D4 resolves the parent question: conditions → trigger → course → verified human cost → short- and long-term consequences | [8] |\n| **Answerability** | Each names its method and data source (source criticism, casualty triangulation, survey/excess models, phase models) | [4][5] |\n| **Alignment** | Every sub-question traces to a component of the parent question; no orphans | [8][12] |\n| **Proportionality** | 16 total, 4 per dimension — within the 3–7 guideline per cluster | [10][12] |\n| **Ordering** | Descriptive (B1, C2) → relational (A2, D2) → causal/evaluative (A4, D4) | [10] |\n\n**Known pitfalls guarded against:** decomposing around convenient data rather than logic [8][13]; hidden causal language in descriptive questions (\"what factors influence…\" smuggles in causal claims) [10][11]; over-decomposition into trivially narrow questions [8]; and framework mismatch (e.g., applying clinical PICO-style decomposition to historical-exploratory work) [7][14].\n\n**Caveats inherited from the historiographical literature:** the neat four-part schema can oversimplify — causes and triggers blur (a trigger \"causes\" only because structures made it potent); periodization of the \"course\" of events is itself interpretive; and causal narrative risks imposing false order on the past [4][5][6]. Decomposition should therefore be treated as provisional and revisited as evidence accumulates [11].\n\n---\n\n## References\n\n[1] Haas, Kates & Aydelotte (eds.), *Reconstruction Following Disaster* (MIT Press, 1977); Sydney Bradshaw Fay, *The Origins of the World War* (1928)\n[2] Iraq Body Count, methodology and documented-casualty database\n[3] Burnham G. et al., \"Mortality after the 2003 invasion of Iraq,\" *The Lancet*, 2006; Fernand Braudel, \"History and the Social Sciences: The Longue Durée\" (1958)\n[4] E.H. Carr, *What Is History?* (1961); Boin, 't Hart, Stern & Sundelius, *The Politics of Crisis Management* (Cambridge UP, 2005)\n[5] John Tosh, *The Pursuit of History*; Quarantelli, \"Disaster Crisis Management,\" *Journal of Management Studies* (1988); OHCHR, \"On My Watch\" (2022)\n[6] WHO, *Global excess deaths associated with COVID-19, 2020–2021* (2022); Anna Green & Kathleen Troup (eds.), *The Houses of History*\n[7] Christopher Clark, *The Sleepwalkers* (2012); Milken Institute School of Public Health (GWU), Hurricane Maria mortality study (2018)\n[8] Chevallier, *Strategic Thinking in Complex Problem Solving* (Oxford); Graham Allison, *Essence of Decision* (1971); Birkland, *After Disaster* (Georgetown UP, 1997)\n[9] McKinsey problem-solving methodology (MECE); Eriksen & Frandsen, *JMLA* (2018); Olson & Drury, \"The Political Aftermath of Hurricanes,\" *IJMED* (2002)\n[10] Creswell & Creswell, *Research Design* (5th ed.); Hicks, Doolittle & Lee, \"SCIM-C\" (2004); Pelling & Dill, \"Disaster Politics,\" *Progress in Human Geography* (2010)\n[11] Crane Brinton, *The Anatomy of Revolution* (1938); Maxwell, *Qualitative Research Design* (3rd ed.)\n[12] O'Leary, *The Essential Guide to Doing Your Research Project*; Paul Pierson, *Politics in Time* (2004)\n[13] Bellingcat, open-source verification guides; Bryman, *Social Research Methods* (5th ed.)\n[14] Comisión de la Verdad y Reconciliación (Peru), final report (2003); Cavallo & Noy, \"Natural Disasters and the Economy — A Survey,\" *IRERE* (2011)\n[16] Hallegatte et al., *Shock Waves* (World Bank, 2016)\n[18] Norris et al., \"60,000 Disaster Victims Speak, Part I,\" *Psychiatry* (2002)\n[19] Kaniasty & Norris, \"A Test of the Social Support Deterioration Model,\" *JPSP* (1993)\n[20] Erikson, *Everything in Its Path* (1976)\n[22] Wisner, Blaikie, Cannon & Davis, *At Risk*, 2nd ed. (2004)\n[23] Aldrich, *Building Resilience* (U Chicago Press, 2012)\n[29] Alexander et al., *Cultural Trauma and Collective Identity* (2004)\n[31] Foote, *Shadowed Ground* (1997)\n[32] Vale & Campanella (eds.), *The Resilient City* (2005)\n[38] UNDRR, *Sendai Framework 2015–2030* (2015)\n[39] UN/EU/World Bank, *Post-Disaster Needs Assessment Guidelines* (2013)\n[43] Alesch, Arendt & Holly, *Managing for Long-Term Community Recovery* (PERI, 2009)"